  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-4x+4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-4x+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}(x-1)-4(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{({x}^{2}-4)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{(x-2)(x+2)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+2}}{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-1)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=1$ and $x=2.$",
